 ## Overview
-3D Texture Mapping is a hardware-accelerated direct volume rendering technique that projects the volumetric dataset onto parallel planar polygons (slices) and relies on the GPU's texture engine to perform trilinear interpolation and resampling [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of software-based ray casting by offloading the computationally intensive sample evaluation to fixed-function hardware, rendering the volume in a ray-parallel fashion [S4]. Unlike sibling methods that may use custom hardware units or specialized acceleration structures, this approach leverages standard 3D texture mapping capabilities available in graphics pipelines, often requiring pre-computed gradients or multi-pass techniques for shading [Parent].
+3D Texture Mapping renders a volumetric dataset by storing it as a 3D texture in GPU memory and resampling it using hardware-accelerated trilinear interpolation on planar polygons [S1, unofficial][S6, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of software ray casting by offloading the expensive interpolation and sampling steps to the graphics hardware's texture engine [S6, unofficial]. Unlike [Shear-Warp](shear-warp.md), which requires a final warp step and performs classification before resampling, this method performs trilinear interpolation followed by classification and compositing, resulting in higher image quality and consistent performance for perspective projections [S1, unofficial].
 
